{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "the social network is a masterful cinematic achievement that dissects the birth of facebook with surgical precision. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the film transforms a story about code and startups into a gripping tragedy of ambition, betrayal, and isolation. the narrative weaves through two depositions, contrasting the legal battles with flashbacks to the chaotic creation of the website at harvard university. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him not as a villain, but as a socially awkward genius whose brilliance is inextricably linked to his inability to connect with others on a human level. the supporting cast is equally impressive, with andrew garfield shining as eduardo saverin, the loyal friend who gets burned, and justin timberlake capturing the charm and underlying ruthlessness of sean parker. the screenplay is a tour de force of rapid-fire dialogue that feels both intellectual and emotionally raw. visually, fincher employs a cold, desaturated color palette and fluid camera movements that mirror the sterile, digital world the characters inhabit. trent reznor and atticus ross provide a haunting, electronic score that underscores the tension and loneliness permeating every scene. the film does not judge its protagonist but instead presents a complex portrait of how the desire for connection can ironically lead to profound alienation. it remains a timeless commentary on the cost of success and the murky ethics of the digital age.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.0074363193}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.00041356293}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.00835604}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.03075233}, {"token": "ful", "logprob": -0.69613016}, {"token": " cinematic", "logpro